<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Loja, Ecuador</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Loja is a city in the southern highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, the capital of the province of the same name. Founded by the Spanish around 1548, it was a centre for the gold mining and cinchona bark trade of the region. During the crisis of 1859, known as the 'terrible year', it briefly had its own government as Ecuador split into rival regimes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/loja-ecuador.jpg" alt="Loja, Ecuador" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David C. S. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Loja.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Loja was definitively founded on 8 December 1548 by the Spanish captain Alonso de Mercadillo in the Cuxibamba valley, after an earlier site of the mid-1540s near La Toma in the Catamayo canyon proved unhealthy. Accounts say the move followed severe earthquake damage and outbreaks of malaria, and that the town took its name from Mercadillo's birthplace, Loja in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. About a century after its founding it was destroyed by an earthquake and rebuilt on its present site. In 1660 the city was consecrated to St. Sebastian to prevent destruction by earthquakes.</p><p>In the colonial period Loja stood on the royal road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, was an administrative hub for the southern highlands and lay near the gold-mining district of Zaruma. From the late 16th century expeditions toward the eastern Andean slopes and the Amazon Basin were organised from Loja, most notably those associated with Juan de Salinas Loyola. Its coat of arms was granted by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> in 1571.</p><p>Two dates mark the city's part in the independence era. Civic commemorations name 18 November 1820 as the political declaration of independence, while the formal Acta de Independencia was recorded on 17 February 1822. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> visited the city in 1822, in the campaigns connected with the consolidation of Gran Colombia. The National University of Loja was founded in 1859, and the Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>, built in 1557, lost most of its Gothic facade in an earthquake in 1867, leaving only its twin spires. A small hydroelectric plant began operating in 1897 and the Loja Hydroelectric Plant was inaugurated in 1899, and the city is widely credited as the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> to have electric lighting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Loja is the capital of Loja Province, in the Cuxibamba valley in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a>, at 2,060 metres above sea level, and has about 203,000 inhabitants. It is known as the Music and Cultural Capital of Ecuador. The cathedral, with 16th-century adobe in its fabric and a building dating from 1838, houses the Virgin of El Cisne for six months of the year; her statue is carried on the backs of the faithful to and from the town of El Cisne, 45 kilometres north. The city lies beside Podocarpus National Park, and the Pan-American Highway runs past it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Loja, Ecuador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
